+# Drag / workspace architecture rework
+
+This source snapshot removes the old `setSuppressSync()` / manual `sync()` mechanism.
+
+## Ownership model
+
+`WindowOrderStore` is model-only: GNOME Shell workspace/window signals update its ordered list and emit `order-changed`. It has no suppression lock and no actor APIs.
+
+`ThumbnailDisplayModeController` reconciles its actor tree from the model. It creates `WindowIconButton` instances and uses `WindowActorRegistry` to keep actor ownership explicit. A leased actor can be detached but cannot be destroyed or adopted while a DND operation is active.
+
+`WindowActorRegistry` keeps the canonical actor for each window and records the active drag lease plus the intended destination workspace.
+
+`WorkspaceWindowMover` performs only the model-level workspace/monitor move and records the target insertion intent.
+
+`WindowReorderDragController` owns DND state, insertion calculation, and the lease lifetime. It does not rebuild display actors. The lease is released only from DND `drag-end`; the drop handler does not call `sync()`.
+
+## Cross-workspace sequence
+
+1. Drag begins; the source icon is leased.
+2. Drop calculates the final insertion index from the current pointer position.
+3. The mover records the target insertion and calls `change_workspace()`.
+4. Source/target order stores process normal GNOME Shell signals.
+5. Display reconciliation is allowed to run, but skips actor work while the dragged actor is leased.
+6. DND emits `drag-end`; the lease is released.
+7. The source display cleans up its stale local mapping; the destination display adopts the same actor in direct mode, or destroys the released actor when collection mode is required.
+
+No timer is used as a synchronization primitive. The existing delayed add remains only for ordinary newly-created windows whose Mutter geometry is not settled when `window-added` fires.
+
+## Synthetic drag actors
+
+The search overlay creates its own temporary drag actor. That actor is not registered as a workspace display actor, so it is not leased; the target display creates/reconciles the canonical `WindowIconButton` normally.
